@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix progression: <http://example.org/orexis/progression#> .
@prefix deliberation: <http://example.org/orexis/deliberation#> .
@prefix assembly: <http://example.org/orexis/assembly#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/deliberation#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/deliberation> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis deliberation" ;
    rdfs:comment """The top layer's own words (#529): the trace — a deliberation, its candidates, what was considered and chosen and why — the budget of worlds, the possible graph, and the extension points answered on the deliberative row.""" .




deliberation:RememberedGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Remembered graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's remembered plans (#469): each a plan that reached its end, lifted filled — its steps as they were walked, with their predictions — and hung on the want it served. Keyed by its regressed precondition (#551) — what its steps' rules read that the chain did not itself produce, computed from the steps and never stored: a pursuit of that want where those facts hold and the first step is on the menu adopts it with no search; where a fact is absent the trace names it; a remembered plan that fails a step is forgotten. A recorded graph, disclosed like the ledger; never a possible world." .
deliberation:PursuedGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WantGraph ; rdfs:label "Pursued graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's pursued WANTS, each derived from a standing desire (#618, an-always-want-is-a-root-and-what-is-pursued-is-derived-from-it): an `orexis:Desire` is a ROOT — the premise of what is pursued, never handed to the search — and what is pursued is a want derived under it: bound `orexis:AtEnd`, `prov:wasDerivedFrom` the root, POINTING at the root's met-test, avoided state and estimate (one owner each) and restating only the root's address, `orexis:about`, which is what the menu joins a want by. Minted here by the derivation the first time the root reads unmet, presented in the root's place by `pursuing()` with the root's own measure, withdrawn when its plan finishes or, by the same derivation from the same rows, when the decomposition stops producing it and no plan is walking it. A record the desire modality projects like the promises. A met root with nothing derived under it is nothing to pursue, and no pass runs for it." .

deliberation:DerivationGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Derivation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What every loaded package's derivation rules READ and WRITE, one `deliberation:Derivation` per INSERT, written whole at every refresh of public knowledge. The scope partition joins predicates wherever one action OR one derivation touches both, and the actions are in the store while the rules are texts on disk — so the edges the rules make are put in the store beside them, and `scope_actions` is a function of the store alone. PUBLIC, because the loaded rule set is the same for everyone reading this store, exactly as the actions graph is; computed, so it is cleared and rewritten rather than added to. A BELIEF graph, as the vocabulary graph is and for the same reason: what a loaded rule reads and writes is a fact about this build, believed, and a public graph that named no modality would leave a reader guessing at its content." .
deliberation:Derivation a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Derivation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One INSERT of one loaded derivation rule, as the edge it makes: the predicates its WHERE reads and the predicates its template writes. Named for the rule file it came from and its place in it, so the same rules write the same text. A side whose predicates cannot be read — a variable predicate, a text the parser refuses — says `deliberation:Anything` and joins every predicate there is, since a rule that might touch anything cannot be proven not to." .
deliberation:reads a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Derivation ; rdfs:label "reads" ;
    rdfs:comment "A predicate this derivation's WHERE reads, or `deliberation:Anything` where the text does not say." .
deliberation:writes a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Derivation ; rdfs:label "writes" ;
    rdfs:comment "A predicate this derivation's template writes, or `deliberation:Anything` where the text does not say." .
deliberation:Anything a owl:Thing ; rdfs:label "Anything" ;
    rdfs:comment "Said where a side's predicates cannot be read off the text: the derivation might read or write any predicate, so it joins them all. relevance's own ANYTHING, in the store." .

##  What the loaded derivations read and write — computed at every refresh of public knowledge
##  from the rule files, and read by `scope_actions`, which names no file.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/derivations> a deliberation:DerivationGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .

deliberation:ScopeGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:WorkingGraph ; rdfs:label "Scope graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "THE STORE's scopes — nobody's, and one graph rather than one per agent, since every agent reading one store would compute the same partition from the same rows — written whole by `scope_actions` from the actions the store holds and the derivations loaded: which predicates some one action or derivation reads or writes together, as `deliberation:Scope` nodes, and which scope each predicate and each action is in. The partition is a function of the actions loaded, which do not change while the agent runs; it was recomputed in Python on every derivation and is data now, so `derive_wants` reads it and clusters the results by it. A working graph: a conclusion, replaced whole per run, carried into no possible world and read beside no state, since none of it is a fact about the world a shape is written against." .
##  The store's scopes — written whole by `scope_actions` from the actions the store holds and
##  the derivations loaded, and read by `derive_wants`. Declared here because it is named: one
##  graph for the store, belonging to no agent, so there is no id to build it from.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/scopes> a deliberation:ScopeGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .

deliberation:Scope a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "A set of predicates joined wherever one action or one derivation reads or writes both, and separate where nothing does — a bulkhead in the vocabulary (knowledge/domain/scope.md). Two wants in different scopes cannot contradict, because no action of one writes a fact the other reads. Named for the graph it is written in and its place in the partition, largest first." .
deliberation:inScope a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range deliberation:Scope ; rdfs:label "in scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "The scope a predicate is in, and the scope an action's reads and writes lie in — by construction one, since an action touching two would have joined them. No domain: said of a property and of an action alike. An action stating no effect is in no scope, as it is on no menu; one whose effect cannot be read is in the one scope that joins everything." .

deliberation:RememberedPlan a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Remembered plan" ;
    rdfs:comment "A method on a want, lifted from a plan that worked (a-remembered-plan-is-a-method-on-the-want): `deliberation:forWant` the want, `deliberation:lifted` its steps in order with their predictions and premises, `deliberation:measuredCost` what the search said it would spend. Its applicability is COMPUTED from the steps — the regressed precondition (#551) — and never stored. Filled, not yet lifted to variables: a world where the precondition holds adopts it outright, and the world corrects it step by step once taken." .
deliberation:lifted a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:RememberedPlan ; rdfs:label "lifted" ;
    rdfs:comment "An rdf:List of the walked steps, each a node with the ledger's own words for a step: what it fills, through what, about what, how much, what it predicts and what it read (its premises)." .
deliberation:measuredCost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:RememberedPlan ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "measured cost" .
deliberation:rememberedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:RememberedPlan ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ; rdfs:label "remembered at" .

deliberation:DeliberationGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PossibleGraph ; rdfs:label "Deliberation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the planner considered on its last pass, per goal: the levers it simulated, the urgency each world would have reached, which one it took and why the others lost.

THIS IS THE RECORD'S ONE EXCEPTION, TAKEN DELIBERATELY AND NOT AN EXEMPTION FROM THE RULE. Possible worlds are computed and dropped, because a stored conclusion can outlive the premise it came from — except where a reader outside the process needs one, and then it is materialised into a graph of its own class, cleared at the start of every pass, carrying PROV to what generated it, never public and never in belief. All four hold here. See a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs, "Possible worlds are computed, not kept".

The reader who needs it cannot get it any other way: pyoxigraph holds an exclusive lock on the belief base, so nothing outside the process can re-run the search to see what it saw. That is the same fact `orexis-ask` exists for.

NOT PUBLIC, and the reason is not tidiness. Which levers an agent weighed and rejected is a disclosure nobody decided to make, and a rival reading it would learn what its neighbour can nearly do.

And nothing may read it BACK: cycle detection lives in the search where it can be reasoned about, not in a graph the agent consults about its own past thinking. A trace that becomes memory is a conclusion feeding a conclusion, which is the failure the computed-not-stored rule exists to prevent.""" .

deliberation:Deliberation a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Deliberation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One pass of the planner, about one goal. Replaced whenever that goal is planned again — the graph holds the last pass and never two, because a trace that outlives its pass is a conclusion sitting beside beliefs it can contradict." .

deliberation:PossibleWorld a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Possible world" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE world a pass IMAGINED — named for what it is, because it never happened: a possible world is what a lever WOULD reach, and a reader meeting `World` beside a belief base has to ask which kind it is. Described where it lives — in the imaginarium, beside the graph that holds its readings and named by it. What the search knew about a world was a Python object and nothing else: its parent, what the path had spent, what the want read there, how far the want still was, whether it had been opened. So a pass could not be stopped and resumed from the store, and nothing could ask what the search had considered while it was considering it. These rows are that object, written down. They die with the pass, as the worlds do." .

deliberation:atInstant a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "at instant" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHEN this world is. A possible world is not only a set of facts, it is a set of facts AT an instant — the pass's clock plus every landing on the path to it — and what a rule is told when it asks what time its own step happens at. Two worlds holding the same facts at different instants are judged against different forecasts. WRITTEN AND NOT DERIVED, which is forced: this engine binds NOTHING for duration arithmetic, so no query can add a path's seconds to the pass's clock, and a reader handed only `deliberation:takes` could not say when the world is. The root's instant is the pass's clock, which is how the clock reaches the store at all." .

deliberation:Weighing a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Weighing" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHAT A PASS WORKED OUT ABOUT ONE WORLD FOR ONE WANT — the relationship between the two, reified because it carries numbers: a thing said not about a subject but about a subject AND what it is being asked against. A world's own facts are true of it whoever asks — where it came from, what the path spent, when it is — but how far a want still is there, what its measure reads, and whether the search has opened it are true of the world AND the want together. One imaginarium holds the worlds of every want in a scope, so a world has as many of these as there are wants asking about it, and writing them on the world would have been a world pretending to have one asker. Dies with the pass, as the worlds do." .

deliberation:weighed a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range deliberation:Weighing ;
    rdfs:label "weighed" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this pass worked out about this world for one of the wants asking about it. One per want, and none before a want has asked: a world nobody has weighed is still a world." .

deliberation:from a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Candidate ; rdfs:range deliberation:PossibleWorld ;
    rdfs:label "from" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world this candidate would be taken IN. THE CANDIDATE'S, not the world's (#747): a possible world points at no other world, only at the candidates that reach it, so the link between two worlds is reified and one world may be approached by different combinations of candidates. It was a world's own property while a world had exactly one way in, which a tree has and a search over states does not — `signature.where` already merges two paths onto one world and simply discards the second arrival." .

deliberation:reachedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range deliberation:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "reached by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate that reached this world — the other half of the reified link, so the edge is followable from either end. Absent on the ROOT, which is where the pass stands and was reached by nothing, and that absence is how a reader finds it. It was `progression:by`, whose domain is `progression:Intention`: a world is not an intention, and the pass graph said so anyway." .

deliberation:spent a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "spent" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the path to this world cost — each step's `orexis:costs` summed. Its own word rather than the action's, which is a SPARQL text to evaluate where this is a number the path accumulated; the core's word has priority (#640)." .

deliberation:remaining a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "remaining" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the want's `orexis:estimates` reads IN this world — how far it still is, in the unit the search spends, never overstated. With `deliberation:spent` it is the A* key the open list is ordered by, which is what lets the frontier be a query rather than a heap." .

deliberation:meets a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range orexis:Want ;
    rdfs:label "meets" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH WANT is met in this world — an achiever, and what a search is looking for. A LINK AND NOT A FLAG, because one imaginarium may hold the worlds of every want in a scope: a world reached by a lever that serves two wants is met for one and unmet for the other, and a boolean could say neither. Said of the world rather than of the pass, so it survives a re-root: whether a want holds somewhere does not change because the agent has since moved. The cheapest achiever is the plan, and the bound the rest are refused against." .

deliberation:fails a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range orexis:Want ;
    rdfs:label "fails" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH WANT was asked of this world and not met. The other half of `deliberation:meets`, and worth writing for the same reason a met-test is worth not running twice: a world judged is a world nobody need judge again — by a pass resumed here, or by a pass for another want sharing the imaginarium. ABSENT ON BOTH COUNTS MEANS THE QUESTION WAS NEVER PUT, which is not the same as the answer being no: a world already reached more cheaply is not re-judged, and one the pass never opened was never asked." .

deliberation:lawful a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "lawful" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the society would accept this world — every shape a package declares about this agent, compiled to the selects whose rows are its violations and asked of the world (#548). ABSENT until asked, which is not the same as false: legality is worked out for a world the search is about to offer, not for every world it makes, and a plan is refused rather than a fork. Said of the world, so it survives a re-root." .

deliberation:minted a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minted" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where this world came in the order the pass made them, counting from the root. THE TIE-BREAK, and not decoration: a want that declares no `orexis:estimates` scores every world alike — same spent, same remaining, same urgency — so what breaks the tie IS the order the search runs in, and arrival order is what makes it go layer by layer rather than dive. It was the heap's mint counter, and moving the open list into the store without it sent a greenhouse pass alphabetically into Dosing until all 128 worlds of its budget were spent, never weighing the Heating branch its plan needed. Re-numbered by a re-root from the order of what it kept, which is the order they were made in." .

deliberation:open a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "open" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether this world is on the frontier — somewhere the search may still open. THE OPEN LIST IS THESE ROWS, ordered by spent plus remaining, which is the A* key the search used to keep a heap for; an iteration needs nothing carried over from the last one but the name the query hands back. OPEN IS NOT MERELY UNEXPANDED and cannot be inferred from `deliberation:expanded`: a world settled with a verdict — met, refused, too dear — is never put on the frontier at all, so its absence is said rather than deduced. Taken back when the world is opened, and put there again by a re-root for every world it kept that was still waiting." .

deliberation:expanded a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Weighing ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "expanded" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether this world has been opened — its levers tried and its children forked. Its ABSENCE is what makes a world part of the frontier, so the open list is `FILTER NOT EXISTS` over these rows ordered by spent-plus-remaining." .

deliberation:signature a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:PossibleWorld ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "signature" ;
    rdfs:comment "What makes two worlds the same world: the canonical facts this one differs by, hashed. A search discards a fork that lands somewhere already seen, and doing that from the store rather than from Python needs the sameness written down. A hash and not the facts, because the facts are the world and this is only its name." .

deliberation:Plan a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Plan" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHAT ONE PASS FOUND, written down: the steps it would take, in order, and how long they come to. It lived only as a Python object handed to the keeper and was then discarded, so nothing outside the process could see what had been decided and the duration the search computed — the summed `orexis:landsAfter` of its path — was recomputed by nobody because it was gone. One per want, named for it and replaced whole, in the deliberation graph, which is cleared at boot because a plan about a world that has moved is stale. ITS STEPS ARE THE LEDGER'S OWN: `progression:Step`, chained by `progression:by` and `progression:then`, each saying what it `progression:fills` and what it goes `progression:through` — the same shape an intention's steps have, so committing to a plan is reading what is already there rather than being handed a Python object across a layer." .

deliberation:forWant a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range orexis:Want ;
    rdfs:label "for want" ;
    rdfs:comment "The want this was found for, or weighed against. Said by three things and so carrying NO domain: a REMEMBERED PLAN, which worked for it once; a PLAN, which is what a pass decided for it; and a WEIGHING, which is what a pass worked out about one world for it. It was declared twice with a domain each, which in RDFS is the intersection of the two — a property nothing could legally carry. A plan is named for its want and replaced whole, so a second pass over the same want leaves one plan and not two — and a reader that holds a want asks for its plan by this rather than by the name, which is a convention." .

deliberation:takes a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "takes" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long this plan comes to, in seconds from its first step to its last landing — the path's summed `orexis:landsAfter`, which the search computes per fork and nothing else can recover, since an action's own is a SPARQL expression about the world the act is taken in. Its own word rather than the action's: what an action declares is a text to evaluate and this is a number, and the core's word has priority (#640). The number a latest start would be subtracted from, where something is not already holding the instant. SAID OF THREE THINGS and meaning one — how long this takes, in seconds: a PLAN takes its path's total, a possible WORLD takes what the path to it came to, and a STEP takes its own, which is the action's `orexis:landsAfter` evaluated in the world it is taken in and is the only place that number survives the pass." .

deliberation:Candidate a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Candidate" ;
    rdfs:comment """One INSTANCE of an action, made while planning: which action, and the variables it was filled with. It is the reified link between possible worlds — `deliberation:from` the world it is taken in, and the world that names it `deliberation:reachedBy` is the one it reaches.

A candidate that reached NOTHING is still a candidate: the search weighs some and passes them over — irrelevant, refused below, too dear — and those have no world at all. That is why it is named from the world it LEAVES rather than the one it reaches, and why there were two node families before #747: one could name a passed-over candidate and the other structurally could not.

The same action filled the same way may appear twice in one pass at different depths, because what a dose is worth depends on where it is taken from — those are two candidates, from two worlds. What it is WORTH to a want is the weighing's, never the candidate's.""" .

deliberation:deliberatedOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ;
    rdfs:label "deliberated on" ;
    rdfs:comment "The goal this pass was about — the shape itself, so a reader can join the trace to the want and see what was being pursued rather than a label for it." .

deliberation:considered a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range deliberation:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "considered" .

deliberation:chose a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range deliberation:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "chose" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate whose world the plan commits to reaching. Absent when the pass took nothing, which is most of them and is an answer rather than a gap: `deliberation:verdict` then says which of the silences it was." .

#  NO DOMAIN, and deliberately: a verdict is said of a PASS (why it ended) and of a CANDIDATE
#  (why it lost), and they are the same idea at two scales. Declaring a domain would make the
#  closure type every candidate a Deliberation, which is worse than saying nothing.
deliberation:verdict a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "verdict" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it ended as it did. On a pass: satisfied, improved, no candidate, exhausted, not better, refused — six answers where a planner returning a move or None could give two. On a candidate: whether it met the goal, improved on standing still, was worse, or landed in a world already seen. On a POSSIBLE WORLD: what stopped the search planning THROUGH it, where something did — and unlike `deliberation:meets` and `deliberation:lawful`, which are about the world, this is about a pass, so a re-root clears it from every world it puts back on the frontier: the resumed pass has not reached them yet." .
deliberation:missing a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "missing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The fact whose absence in the present made a remembered plan inapplicable here (#551), stated as the signature states it — so the trace says which fact, not only that one was." .

deliberation:wouldReach a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "would reach" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world this lever would have made. The comparison the whole search turns on: a candidate is worth taking only if this is lower than where the agent already stands." .

deliberation:standsAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stands at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world the agent is actually in — what every candidate is measured against, kept so a reader need not recompute it to understand the verdicts." .

deliberation:wouldTake a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Candidate ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "would take" ;
    rdfs:comment "The action this candidate would take. NOT `progression:by`, whose domain is `progression:Intention` — spelling a weighed lever with the word for a carried-out one would have the closure type every candidate a commitment, which is the exact distinction a trace exists to keep." .

deliberation:atDepth a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "at depth" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many steps into the plan this candidate sits. Kept because a lever rejected at depth 0 and the same lever rejected at depth 1 are different findings — the second says a chain was tried." .

deliberation:tookSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "took seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long the pass took. The one figure a trace could not already answer, and the one most likely to change this design: a pass validates a shape per candidate, and `series()` re-plans EVERY goal on every reporting tick, so an agent's dominant cost may turn out to be the instrumentation asking it what it wants. Recorded from inside the pass rather than timed by a caller, because a caller would be timing the trace write as well and reporting the observer's cost as the observed's." .
deliberation:keptWorlds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "kept worlds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many imagined worlds this pass began with, kept from the pass before and re-rooted on the present (#553), the present itself among them — one is a kept leaf the present was found in; zero is a pass that began from nothing, which is every pass whose present matched no kept world or whose invariant half had moved." .
deliberation:surprise a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "surprise" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a present that matched no kept world meant (#570): `withheld`, a world the last pass chose not to imagine for budget or cost and found once the old root's withheld rows were forked; or `exogenous`, a world no lever of ours produces — another agent acted, the environment moved, or an action has an outcome it does not declare. Followed by the facts, within the want's view, the present holds beyond every imagined world and lacks. Absent where the present was a kept world or the pass began from nothing for another reason." .

deliberation:asOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "as of" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the pass ran. A trace is about a moment — the readings it weighed were the ones held then — and a reader who cannot tell how old it is cannot tell whether it explains what the agent is doing now or what it was doing before the last reading arrived." .

deliberation:blind a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "blind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether some lever was passed over for want of a stated effect, leaving the pass unable to claim it looked at everything. A verdict of `no candidate` beside this being true means the search saw part of the menu — which is why the deliberator defers to the reflex rather than reporting that nothing helps." .

deliberation:judgedThrough a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "judged through" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way decided whether the want was met in each world weighed (#502): the select compiled from its shape, the pattern it authors, the record of what was discharged, or a module's measure — or none, where a remembered plan was adopted without a pass. Said on every pass rather than left to be inferred from what else the trace carries, because a kind said by absence is a kind two readers disagree about." .

deliberation:judgedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain deliberation:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "judged by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The SELECT text the pass judged every candidate world by, where the way was a text: the select the kernel compiled from the want's shape (#497, #499), or the pattern the want authors. An EXPLANATION of what a pass ran, never a property of the shape — a shape's sh:sparql slot is a constraint the judge would conjoin and report twice, and a stored copy would outlive the shape it was compiled from. Absent where the way is the record or a measure." .

#  HOW MUCH ONE PASS MAY IMAGINE, in worlds — the ceiling on what a deliberation spends, stated
#  in the unit the search actually spends: a fork in the imaginarium, whose cost per world the
#  runbook measures against the mutable slice. It was a DEPTH, a constant in the planner, and
#  under a breadth-first search depth was a ceiling on compute; best-first (#492) it bounds
#  only how far ahead a plan reaches, which nothing needs — a plan is re-derived every pass
#  and only its head is acted on. A pick like the patience: authored per world, never moved
#  by the agent unless a mandate says so, and the engine's own default where none is stated.
#  See decisions/a-pass-is-budgeted-in-worlds.md.
deliberation:budgetWorlds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "budget, in worlds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many possible worlds one deliberation may fork before it answers with the best it has found. The search is anytime — it holds the best world seen and stops here — so a spent budget answers with progress, never with nothing. NOT a depth: a world's cost is what its mutable slice makes it, measured, and a sovereign states this in that unit. An OPINION of the sovereign's about this agent, authored in its beliefs; the agent does not move it." .

